* START-HISTORY:
* 14 Aug 26 Windows port - Remove-LocalUser replaces userdel
* 21 Aug 26 Windows port - and it takes the user PROFILE with it now
* 27 Aug 26 Windows port - and the ProfileList REGISTRY ENTRY even when
*           Remove-CimInstance fails.  PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md 32.
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    x = !delete_user(username)
*
*    Delete a Windows local user.  Returns true on success; on failure sets
*    STATUS() and returns false.
*
*      0 success
*      1 the account could not be removed
*      2 the name is not a valid OS user name
*      5 access denied: SD is not running elevated
*      6 the account went, the profile DIRECTORY did not - see below
*      7 the account went, the ProfileList REGISTRY ENTRY did not - see below
*
*    THE PROFILE GOES TOO, AND THIS REVERSES WHAT THIS COMMENT USED TO SAY.
*    It said the directory was left alone because "C:\Users\<name> is the
*    person's own data and removing an SD account is not a reason to destroy
*    it".  That reasoning is sound for an account SD merely BORROWED and does
*    not apply to one SD CREATED - and DELACC is the only caller, which asks
*    !is_sd_user first and never gets here for a borrowed account.
*
*    WHAT THE OLD BEHAVIOUR COST, measured 21 Aug 2026: TWENTY-SIX orphaned
*    profiles under C:\Users going back to 14 Aug, one per account-creating
*    verifier run.  Every one had a live ProfileList registry entry with no
*    account behind it.
*
*    AND THE REGISTRY ENTRY IS THE HALF THAT BITES.  Removing the DIRECTORY
*    alone leaves the entry, and Windows then honours it the next time an
*    account of that name appears - by putting the new profile at
*    C:\Users\<name>.<COMPUTERNAME> instead.  That is where sdacct19.GITORLI,
*    sdacct20.GITORLI, sdacct27.GITORLI and sdsshprobe.GITORLI came from.
*    Remove-CimInstance on Win32_UserProfile takes both halves; Remove-Item
*    would take the worse one.
*
*    27 Aug 26 - AND WHEN Remove-CimInstance FAILS IT TAKES NEITHER, WHICH IS
*    THE CASE THIS ROUTINE USED TO LEAVE BEHIND.  The catch below exited 6 and
*    stopped, so a profile whose hive was still loaded - the ordinary state
*    after a session - kept its registry entry, and the next account of that
*    name got the suffixed home.  MEASURED 27 Aug 2026, PRE_RELEASE_FIXES.md
*    32: C:\Users\sdsshb48 AND C:\Users\sdsshb48.GITORLI on the same host, with
*    53 stale ProfileList entries for accounts Get-LocalUser says do not exist.
*    THE SYMPTOM WAS NOT A MISSING DIRECTORY: it was ssh public-key
*    authentication refused, because sshd reads authorized_keys under the
*    profile Windows assigns and the key was under the other one.
*
*    SO THE REGISTRY ENTRY IS NOW REMOVED IN ITS OWN RIGHT, after the CIM call
*    rather than instead of it.  Remove-CimInstance is still tried first and is
*    still the right tool - it takes both halves and it does the bookkeeping -
*    but its failure no longer decides anything.
*
*    THE SID IS READ BEFORE THE ACCOUNT IS DELETED, and the order is not
*    incidental: a profile is keyed by SID, and once the account is gone the
*    name can no longer be resolved to one.  THE PROFILE PATH IS READ BEFORE
*    THE ENTRY IS REMOVED, for the same kind of reason: the entry is the only
*    place that records where the directory is.
*
*    STATUS 6 AND 7 BOTH MEAN THE ACCOUNT WENT AND SOMETHING OF THE PROFILE
*    DID NOT.  ***NEITHER OF THEM IS HARMLESS, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS
*    COMMENT SAID 6 WAS.***  It read "it costs disk and nothing else - the
*    registry entry is gone, so an account created with this name later gets
*    its proper home".  MEASURED FALSE THE SAME DAY, on the install that
*    carried the registry fix: delete.account b49home removed the entry
*    correctly, left the DIRECTORY, and the recreated account still landed at
*    C:\Users\b49home.GITORLI - because Windows will not put a new profile
*    where a directory already sits either.
*
*      6  the DIRECTORY is left.  A later account of the same name is pushed
*         to a suffixed home, exactly as 7 does it.
*      7  the REGISTRY ENTRY is left.  Same consequence by the other route.
*
*    BOTH HALVES ARE THEREFORE REMOVED IN THEIR OWN RIGHT, and the two statuses
*    survive only to say WHICH one could not be removed, because the way out
*    differs: a directory needs deleting, a registry entry needs clearing.
*
*    Neither is the same failure as 1 and the caller says so: the account is
*    deleted either way, so re-running would report "does not exist" and never
*    retry the profile.  gplbld/clean-test-profiles.ps1 sweeps what is left.
*
*    BOTH HALVES ARE MEASURED RATHER THAN INFERRED FROM WHICH CALL THREW.  A
*    thrown Remove-CimInstance does not tell you what it managed to remove
*    first, so the script tests for each half after the attempt instead of
*    concluding from the exception.
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
function delete_user(username)
$catalog !delete_user

deffun valid_os_name(name) calling '!valid_os_name'

   user_deleted = @false
   set.status 0

   if not(valid_os_name(username)) then
      set.status 2
      return user_deleted
   end

   ps = 'if (-not (New-Object Security.Principal.WindowsPrincipal('
   ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent())).IsInRole('
   ps := '[Security.Principal.WindowsBuiltInRole]::Administrator)) { exit 5 }; '
   ps := '$n = "' : username : '"; '
   * The SID FIRST - see the note above.  A profile is keyed by SID and the
   * name cannot be resolved to one after the account has gone.
   ps := 'try { $sid = (Get-LocalUser -Name $n -ErrorAction Stop).SID.Value } '
   ps := 'catch { exit 1 }; '
   * The profile's registry entry, named while the SID is still in hand.
   ps := '$key = Join-Path '
   ps := '"HKLM:\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\ProfileList" $sid; '
   * AND ITS DIRECTORY, READ BEFORE ANYTHING IS REMOVED.  That entry is the
   * only record of where the directory is, and it is about to go.  The value
   * may be a REG_EXPAND_SZ, so it is expanded rather than trusted as written -
   * an unexpanded %SystemDrive% would make Test-Path answer false and report a
   * directory that is still there as gone.
   ps := '$dir = ""; '
   ps := 'try { $dir = (Get-ItemProperty -LiteralPath $key -Name ProfileImagePath '
   ps := '-ErrorAction Stop).ProfileImagePath } catch { }; '
   ps := 'if ($dir -ne "") { $dir = [Environment]::ExpandEnvironmentVariables($dir) }; '
   ps := 'try { Remove-LocalUser -Name $n -ErrorAction Stop } catch { exit 1 }; '
   * An account that never signed in has no profile, and that is not a
   * failure: Where-Object simply matches nothing and Remove-CimInstance is
   * handed an empty pipeline.  Special profiles are excluded on principle
   * rather than because one could match - a local account's SID never is one.
   *
   * 27 Aug 26 - THIS CATCH NO LONGER EXITS, and that one word is the fix.
   * It used to exit 6 here, which left the registry entry behind with the
   * directory - see the note above.  The failure is allowed to fall through
   * so the entry is dealt with in its own right below.
   ps := 'try { Get-CimInstance Win32_UserProfile | '
   ps := 'Where-Object { $_.SID -eq $sid -and -not $_.Special } | '
   ps := 'Remove-CimInstance -ErrorAction Stop } catch { }; '
   ps := 'if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $key) { try { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $key '
   ps := '-Recurse -Force -ErrorAction Stop } catch { } }; '
   * 27 Aug 26 - AND THE DIRECTORY IN ITS OWN RIGHT TOO, for the reason
   * MEASURED an hour after the registry half was fixed: a directory left at
   * C:\Users\<name> makes Windows give the NEXT account of that name the
   * suffixed home just as surely as a stale registry entry does.  Both halves
   * cause the same symptom, so removing only one of them fixes only half the
   * defect - see the note above.
   ps := 'if (($dir -ne "") -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $dir)) { '
   ps := 'try { Remove-Item -LiteralPath $dir -Recurse -Force -ErrorAction Stop } catch { } }; '
   * Test for each half rather than conclude from which call threw: a thrown
   * Remove-CimInstance does not say what it removed before it threw.
   ps := 'if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $key) { exit 7 }; '
   ps := 'if (($dir -ne "") -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $dir)) { exit 6 }; '
   ps := 'exit 0'

   os.execute ps

   stat = os.error()
   begin case
      case stat = 0
         user_deleted = @true

      * 21 Aug 26 - THE ACCOUNT WENT AND THE PROFILE DID NOT, which is a
      * SUCCESS with a warning attached rather than a failure.  Reporting it
      * as a failure would be wrong twice: the account really is gone, and the
      * caller would offer to retry something that can never succeed now,
      * because the name no longer resolves to a SID.
      *
      * 27 Aug 26 - AND 7 IS THE SAME SHAPE FOR THE SERIOUS HALF.  Kept as a
      * separate status rather than folded into 6 because the caller's warning
      * has to differ: 6 costs disk, 7 moves a recreated account's home.
      case stat = 6 or stat = 7
         user_deleted = @true
         set.status stat

      case 1
         set.status stat
   end case

return user_deleted
end

* END-CODE
